  ],
  "text": "The Arctic tern flies pole to pole every year. Seventy thousand kilometres. Over a thirty year life, that is three round trips to the Moon. A monarch butterfly cannot even finish its own migration. It takes several generations. Nobody who sets off ever arrives. A bar tailed godwit flies for eleven days without landing once. A salmon finds the exact stream it hatched in, years later. The distance was never the hard part. Finding the way is. How does something with a brain the size of a pea know where it is going? The answer is that they read a world you cannot sense at all. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
